{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1543770-on-the-euphoria-of-the-diablerie","id":1543770,"title":"On the Euphoria of the Diablerie","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2024-10-14T11:08:18-07:00","id":1543770,"title":"On the Euphoria of the Diablerie","content":"Hi, everyone. I hope you're all doing fine. <br /> <br /> I'm having trouble trying to find information regarding the euphoria/dizziness caused by commiting <i>diablerie.</i> I remember I read about it eons ago, but I'm not finding it, it seems they didn't bother to add this information in Dark Agees Vampire v20, because I swear I can't find it. So, does anyone have any idea about the system? I know that commiting <i>diablerie</i> makes one euphoric for a given set of time and during this time, it's harder to focus, etc. I'm looking for this information in all my books, but until now, I had no luck. I'd be very thankful if anyone could point me where do I find such information. Thank you very much!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-10-14T11:29:49-07:00","id":1543771,"title":"","content":"Oh, so I found some information on The 20th Anniversaty Edition, on p. 294, thanks to one of my very favourite books V20 has published, that is A Guide to the Tal'Mahe'Ra. Still, it's only about getting addicted to <i>diablerie</i>, I might be crazy, but I remember having read something some eons ago about th system for the distraction caused by the euphoria. But, since I ST since I was literally a child, some 20 years ago and have read countless books, I'm definitely going to have a hard time trying to find it, if anyone can find the specifics, or know it by heart, I'd be glad to read.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-10-14T16:10:48-07:00","id":1543789,"title":"","content":"You could be thinking of Diablerie: Mexico or the other sourcebook in that series, Diablerie: Britain. While Diablerie as a concept was mentioned in the corebook, a lot of the mechanics and lore surrounding it was contained for the first time in Diablerie: Mexico. It has over a half dozen pages on diablerie itself, and while various bits have probably been added to other sourcebooks based on that, there's always a lot that gets left behind and not mentioned again.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-10-14T16:25:34-07:00","id":1543793,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1543770-on-the-euphoria-of-the-diablerie?p=1543789#post1543789\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You could be thinking of Diablerie: Mexico or the other sourcebook in that series, Diablerie: Britain. While Diablerie as a concept was mentioned in the corebook, a lot of the mechanics and lore surrounding it was contained for the first time in Diablerie: Mexico. It has over a half dozen pages on diablerie itself, and while various bits have probably been added to other sourcebooks based on that, there's always a lot that gets left behind and not mentioned again.</div> </div> </div> </div>Thanks for the reply, is this book the one where the fabled Samuel Haight goes to Mexico City?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-10-14T19:11:18-07:00","id":1543810,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Herr Meister</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1543770-on-the-euphoria-of-the-diablerie?p=1543793#post1543793\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Thanks for the reply, is this book the one where the fabled Samuel Haight goes to Mexico City?</div> </div> </div> </div>That would be <b>Chaos Factor</b> the final cross splat book of the Haight saga. <b>Chaos Factor</b> introduces Nergal/Huitzilopotchli (4th gen Baali) and the idea that he's been pulling a portion of the early Sabbat strings through Melinda Gabraith. He also controls a massive Wyrmish and Nephandic Hive located under Mexico City. Haight goes there thinking Nergal is an Antedeluvian and that he has accrued enough power to subjugate him to steal his vitae and use it to become more powerful (at this point he's a vitae addled pseudo awakened mage kinfolk turned Skindancer Garou....) It doesn't end well for him.<br /> <br /> The <b>Diablerie: Mexico</b> book was a more self contained adventure where a coterie goes on a dungeon crawl (its very DnD-ish) to find a methuselah in an Aztec temple to diablerize with a Thaumaturgical ritual that could possibly spread the diablerie to all that members simultaneously and lower <i>everyone of the coterie's </i>generation. The ritual was called the <i>Ritual of the Bitter Rose.</i> Its one of those early books were the stories were more adventure oriented rather than political and personal horror focused.<br /> <br /> I'll take a look through my collection and see if I can't find specific mentions of the euphoria.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":26219,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26219-aydoo","user":"Aydoo","date":".  Text formating 10-14-2024, 11:18 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26219-aydoo","name":"Aydoo","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"83"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-10-14T20:16:44-07:00","id":1543820,"title":"","content":"From<b> Vampire the Dark Ages Core Revised pg 259:</b><br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> In the immediate aftermath of the Amaranth, the diablerist is filled with an orgasmic sense of joy and power, exulting in the experience of draining another\u2019s soul. The emotions of this act are often enough to trigger a frenzy, so the diablerist must make a Self-Control or Instinct roll against a difficulty equal to 10 - his Road rating. Where a Cainite diablerizes a vampire of equal or higher generation, this \u201crush\u201d is the only effect of the Amaranth, a sensation to which some Cainites become addicted. The true power of the act comes from diablerizing a Cainite of lower gen-eration, for example an 11th-generation vampire drinking the blood and soul of a member of the ninth generation </div> </div> </div>As stated previously <b>Diablerie: Mexico</b> has several pages dedicated to describing the act itself, but here's a snippet from <b>pg 11</b>:<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> So overwhelming are the sensations associated with Diablerie that they can have very real, physical consequences. For the Diabolist, there is a chance of Frenzy, which arises from the sheer magnitude of the experience. To avoid Frenzy, the Diabolist must make a Self-Control roll against a difficulty of 12 minus the Generation of the Vessel. --- If the Diabolist fails the roll, he suffers Madness (the usual result of a Conscience Frenzy)l so great was the ecstasy that the Vampire will try anything to regain it. If the Self-Control roll is botched, however, the Vampire gains a Terror Derangement; the experience was so literally over-whelming that it has shaken the Diabolist's sanity.<br /> <br /> Further, the Diabolist must make a seperate Self-Control roll against a difficulty of 7 minus the Vessel's Generation. If this roll is failed or botched, the Diabolist gains the Derangement, Delusions of Grandeur. This is in addition to any Derangement gained through Frenzy. </div> </div> </div><b>VtM Core Revised</b> has rules on diablerie on <b>pgs 224-226</b> but only really talks about it from the mechanical aspects and doesn't mention the euphoria. Likewise <b>VtM core 2nd Edition </b>has very little given to the act on <b>pgs 187-188</b>.<b> VtM Storyteller's Handbook pg 75-77</b> talks about diablerie from a plotpoint and directly references back to <b>Diablerie: Mexico</b>.<br /> <br /> Aside from the <b>20th Editions </b>which you already mentioned and the <b>VtM 1st Edition</b> which I don't have, there isn't much other information that I could find on cursory glance unless its really hidden in some obscure book. I checked all the core books (including Victorian Age) and Player/Storyteller Handbook type supplements.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26219-aydoo","name":"Aydoo","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"83"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-10-14T20:41:22-07:00","id":1543824,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aydoo</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1543770-on-the-euphoria-of-the-diablerie?p=1543810#post1543810\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> That would be <b>Chaos Factor</b> the final cross splat book of the Haight saga. <b>Chaos Factor</b> introduces Nergal/Huitzilopotchli (4th gen Baali) and the idea that he's been pulling a portion of the early Sabbat strings through Melinda Gabraith. He also controls a massive Wyrmish and Nephandic Hive located under Mexico City. Haight goes there thinking Nergal is an Antedeluvian and that he has accrued enough power to subjugate him to steal his vitae and use it to become more powerful (at this point he's a vitae addled pseudo awakened mage kinfolk turned Skindancer Garou....) It doesn't end well for him.<br /> <br /> The <b>Diablerie: Mexico</b> book was a more self contained adventure where a coterie goes on a dungeon crawl (its very DnD-ish) to find a methuselah in an Aztec temple to diablerize with a Thaumaturgical ritual that could possibly spread the diablerie to all that members simultaneously and lower <i>everyone of the coterie's </i>generation. The ritual was called the <i>Ritual of the Bitter Rose.</i> Its one of those early books were the stories were more adventure oriented rather than political and personal horror focused.<br /> <br /> I'll take a look through my collection and see if I can't find specific mentions of the euphoria.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, I got the two confused. I remember Samuel Haight arc, since Valkenburg, all my friends were fans of the character and even wanted me to use him in one of our stories. Diablerie: Mexico is the one with that strange methuselah, I remember it vaguely, I've read these books many years ago, but I remember Diablerie: Mexico had som interesting rituals and IIRC had House Goratrix involved in the plot, am I right? It's been such a long time, I remember about a ritual that makes one immune to ranged attacks, that creates a sort of force field, if I'm not mistaken. I can't recall it all right now, but I have those books somewhere around here.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-10-14T20:43:28-07:00","id":1543825,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aydoo</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1543770-on-the-euphoria-of-the-diablerie?p=1543820#post1543820\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">From<b> Vampire the Dark Ages Core Revised pg 259:</b><br /> <br /> <br /> As stated previously <b>Diablerie: Mexico</b> has several pages dedicated to describing the act itself, but here's a snippet from <b>pg 11</b>:<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>VtM Core Revised</b> has rules on diablerie on <b>pgs 224-226</b> but only really talks about it from the mechanical aspects and doesn't mention the euphoria. Likewise <b>VtM core 2nd Edition </b>has very little given to the act on <b>pgs 187-188</b>.<b> VtM Storyteller's Handbook pg 75-77</b> talks about diablerie from a plotpoint and directly references back to <b>Diablerie: Mexico</b>.<br /> <br /> Aside from the <b>20th Editions </b>which you already mentioned and the <b>VtM 1st Edition</b> which I don't have, there isn't much other information that I could find on cursory glance unless its really hidden in some obscure book. I checked all the core books (including Victorian Age) and Player/Storyteller Handbook type supplements.</div> </div> </div> </div>Thank you very much for your research! I also couldn't find anything more specific, I think I might be mistaken, I don't really know to be honest. In any case, thanks again for the help! I'm going to try to find it, but I'm sincerely beginning to think I might have been mistaken about it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-10-14T21:02:33-07:00","id":1543827,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Herr Meister</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1543770-on-the-euphoria-of-the-diablerie?p=1543825#post1543825\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Thank you very much for your research! I also couldn't find anything more specific, I think I might be mistaken, I don't really know to be honest. In any case, thanks again for the help! I'm going to try to find it, but I'm sincerely beginning to think I might have been mistaken about it.</div> </div> </div> </div>No problem. I own like 95% of the books White Wolf ever published up to 5th Edition Core. There is definitely precedent with the idea of diablerie being euphoric as was stated in the Dark Ages book, but the more modern books seem to just ignore it in favor of storyteller fiat. Diablerie: Mexico is definitely the most comprehensive description of all the books I went through. The original book is described <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Awakening:_Diablerie_Mexico\" target=\"_blank\">here</a> The reprint is in a book called <b>VtM Diablerie</b> which can be described <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Diablerie_(book)\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26219-aydoo","name":"Aydoo","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"83"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-10-14T22:06:01-07:00","id":1543831,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aydoo</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1543770-on-the-euphoria-of-the-diablerie?p=1543827#post1543827\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> No problem. I own like 95% of the books White Wolf ever published up to 5th Edition Core. There is definitely precedent with the idea of diablerie being euphoric as was stated in the Dark Ages book, but the more modern books seem to just ignore it in favor of storyteller fiat. Diablerie: Mexico is definitely the most comprehensive description of all the books I went through. The original book is described <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Awakening:_Diablerie_Mexico\" target=\"_blank\">here</a> The reprint is in a book called <b>VtM Diablerie</b> which can be described <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Diablerie_(book)\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>.</div> </div> </div> </div>Once again, thank you very much! I'm still unable to find the system for this euphoric state, as I said, unfortunately. I also own about as many WW books, although probably less than 95% at this point, since I kind of stopped acquiring it recently, I haven't read W5, I was very disappointed with many of the changes (many utterly nonsensical) to the setting and powers in V5, but I was at least happy that they finally decided to go Requiem and abolish celerity multiple actions, something that totally broke the system and something I decided to abolish since about 15 years ago, as it was obvious to me that the system can't handle it. Then, I colleague of mine mentioned that Werewolves in W5 can use Rage to make it work like Celerity (like in the old days) and then I'm still praying that he's incorrect, but since I houserule so many things at this point, I shouldn't mind it that much, but I pray that he's incorrect, because it would be the most idiotic idea one could ever conceive, giving that they changed celerity completely. But in any case, back to topic, I know the euphoria is mentioned in some books, but what I'm falling to find is it's system, besides the frenzy, that is described in Diablerie:Mexico, for instance.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-10-14T22:24:39-07:00","id":1543833,"title":"","content":"Think I found it or something similar. <b>Dirty Secrets of the Black Hand pg 41</b><br /> <img title=\"image.png\" data-tempid=\"temp_1699_1728969573757_606\" data-align=\"none\" data-size=\"ful